    "turn_text": "to be a happy channel that maybe you can just turn to a happy channel. Right. So everything sucks sucks. The world's gonna end. Terrible, terrible. Oh, the happy channel. Yeah. Senator Paul welcome back to the show glad to be with Matt and I know there's lots of activity on the Hill So we're gonna we're gonna sort of rifle through some things, but we had to get a proper bourbon before we tell cheers Cheers Cheers to all the depressing things that are happening in Washington DC right now. Oh, yes If we could just get the legislature out of town then things will be happier Maybe yeah, like could we let to celebrate Festivus every day of the year? Yeah, maybe, you know, this will be a sad year, you know, without Frank Constanza around, you know, these gone. But, uh, no, I think it was Twain said the dangerous time, you know, is when legislators, you know, the only time you're safe is when they're not in session. So August and some conservatives and even libertarians get this wrong. Y'all need to stay in place in August and work because we have so many problems. It's like, don't you realize we'll, we'll misdiagnose the problems, come up with the wrong solution and then waste more of the taxpayer money. That's the solution to everything. Yeah. I feel like at this rate, every month that Congress is in costs us another trillion dollars in deficit spending. Yeah. In the old days, it only would cost you a hundred billion. The Congress was in session doing foolish things, throwing money at problems, thinking they were the answer, everything. Only a cool hundred billion, maybe in a month. That would have been a lot, actually. Do you remember, this is a long, long time ago, in 2008, when there was a recession, and do you remember how conservatives were outraged at both Republicans and Democrats, but you know how much they spent? I think it was $800 billion at one time. That pales in comparison, that's a jump change. Now, we don't even consider, not we. I don't want to be part of the way Congress doesn't even consider their attempting to fix your problem unless they give you a trillion. And that might not be enough. Yeah, it's like the zeros don't seem to matter anymore. You were so let's let's start with something that you just did you you were at a hearing this morning speaking to the new special assistant for Venezuela at the State Department, L.A. Abrams. I take it you guys don't always see eye to eye on things. Well, I wanted to make sure he knew because not one person mentioned it. I wanted to make sure someone in the country knew that the person they're supporting down there is also a socialist. Now, he may not at this point be an authoritarian, but my point to him was is that if you're going to send troops down there, you're going to ask my kids or your kids to go to war in Venezuela for freedom or freedom fighters, make sure that you tell them we're choosing one form of socialism over another, And that socialism was the problem down there. And he sloughed it off and he just sort of dismissed it and said, no, it's authoritarianism. And this is sort of the same view I got on the view when I went on there is the so-called Republican Ananovaro saying, Maduro's a thug. He's a murderer. And I was like, yes, and a socialist. And maybe there's a correlation between socialism and thuggery. Yeah, his answer was kind of surprising because he essentially said, I don't have a problem with socialism. Socialist countries are not really part of US foreign policy. It's just bad socialists. And your argument is it's a feature, not a bug. Yeah and you know we went over this and you and I have talked about our book you know the case against socialism and in it we talk about whether or not it's in inevitability or whether it's happenstance. Do we just get unlucky that every time we finally get socialism we don't get a benign form or somehow it goes from so-called benign or democratic socialism but it seems like time and time again we wind up with authoritarianism And I don't know if it was Hayek or who it was, but people have mentioned that maybe it's not an accident that actually if you're going to take people's stuff, you know, you're not supposed to take people's stuff. If you're going to take it from them that eventually they resist and they require someone increasingly ruthless to take people's stuff from them. Yeah, and it's like the common left narrative about Joseph Stalin and Applebaum wrote a book about this, and I might have mentioned it before, but this idea that Stalin was an accident that such a murderous individual got into power, that wasn't supposed to be part of the plan. And she argues that, no, no, he's actually behaving exactly how an authoritarian socialist would behave if you want to control power and force people to do things they don't want to do. Right. And some people talk about it being the complete form of socialism. The more complete you want socialism to be, the more force has to be used. So if I want sort of a little bit of socialism or interventionism like we have in our country where I'm just going to tax you or regulate you in a way and I may find you, but I'm not going to actually take your house or take your car literally or say you can't go to church or you can't do this. Um, the amount of form doesn't take much as much aggression. I can threaten you with jail and that may be enough. But in a society where they're actually taking your property and taking your house and you have more complete socialism, the more complete you get towards the state owning things, the more ruthlessness it takes. And, but it's, you know, there's Stalin and then there's Hitler, then there's Mao, then there's Pol Pot, you know, and then there's Chavez, a lot of the left in our country love Chavez. Um, still some like Maduro, frankly, but they, They liked him, but they said, oh, I don't know what, you know, oh, now he's an authoritarian. He's not a socialist anymore. And they blame it on him being a thug, not admitting that there's a correlation. I take it. There's still a drumbeat within certain wings of the Trump administration for regime change in Venezuela. Well, this is the interesting thing I ask Eliot Abrams is I said, well, and I like to remind most of his, the president's appointees that the president has been very consistent and very suspicious of regime change. He thinks it's the worst geopolitical mistake we made in the Middle East was toppling Saddam Hussein and taking Iraq. And I remind him of that. I said, some of you guys and I meant him are now rattling, you know, the cages to take over Iran. And it's like, well, the reason Iran stronger is because you got rid of their main nemesis Hussein. You change the balance of power. You created a government where there was no government and the out of the chaos lo and behold sprang terrorism and they just don't want to hear it but yeah if he had his way he would he would be for regime change but the question is is will they be better off if they still have price controls inflation and they spend money that they don't have which are all sort of forms and aspects of socialism, will they be any better off because they have elections? See, they're convinced, oh, if you have elections, everything, but that sounds kind of like, because it sounds like Bernie Sanders to me, or AOC, they're following socialism as long as there's a democracy, and it's like, I guess so is Eliot Abrams. Yeah. So another, like, and he refused to answer your question as to whether or not he supported the president, presuming the Trump administration's policy that Iraq was a mistake, But the president has been trying pretty much from day one to get our troops out of Afghanistan. And yet, we're still in Afghanistan. And I know you've been pushing him and Congress, but are we going to get out of Afghanistan? I talked to him all the time about it. I think the president sincerely wants to get out. Libertarians are frustrated because they're like, well, he hadn't. And the numbers are maybe slightly less in Afghanistan, but the numbers are troops in Saudi Arabia are actually more. And it's been sort of an inconsistent. It hasn't really been non interventionism or less intervention. It's been sort of a, you know, a middling sort of policy. I do think, though, from a libertarian perspective, I try to look at what are the bright sides and influence that. So, for example, I think as interactions with Iran, I haven't agreed with everything. I didn't agree necessarily with killing Soleimani. I thought that was an act of war. Yet, in the midst of all that, when he could have attacked Moore, when they shot down a drone and they fired on ships, he did show restraint that I don't think a George Bush would have shown. I don't think a Joe Biden would have shown that restraint. We never have who we think is perfect in a position, but there are aspects of the President's foreign policy that do lean in the direction of libertarianism. I mean, I do think that that he's been not served well by the and you've, of course, have said this, but, you know, hiring John Bolton and hiring, hiring Elliot Abrams. I mean, I told you I don't want to hear that name ever again. Don't ever met now. I'm just kidding. But yeah, when I think. No, when I think of John Bolton, I think, you know, I'm talking to John Bolton, Jr. and Elliott Abrams. They're both big regime change people. And I worry, why does the president pick people who don't agree with one fundamental thing? It's like, if I were hiring and President Trump appointed me to hire his foreign policy team, which frankly he should have, but had he asked me to do that, the first question I would ask you when you came into interview is, do you agree with the president that the Iraq war was a mistake? If you don't, goodbye. Because why would I even start to talk with somebody if you can't agree on that fundamental promise? I've had people come in who are big Republicans and they're being appointed to positions and they have to come through the Senate for religious committee and I warn them in advance. I'm I'm letting you know so you can prepare. I'm going to ask you do you agree with the president on this? Most of them still fumble because they actually don't and that's the problem. He's not getting changed because he's appointing people who are from the neoconservative world who don't agree with trying to leave Afghanistan. They want to stay. Yeah. Are there Democrats, it's political season. So a lot of partisans will go back into their corner. And that's usually particularly true with President Trump. But are there Democrats who you would consider steadfast allies on getting out of these permanent wars? there are and there are some that will rise above politics and right now they have to because they have to rise up and actually agree with the president on this or at least talk about something the president's talking about uh... row kana i think has been good and consistent against war he and i and others signed a letter some others of the progressive caucus and the libertarian wing signed a letter to the president of saying that uh... not getting more engaged in syria was a good idea and we've also signed letters on afghanistan On my committee, the Senate Foreign Relations Committee, I talk with Tom Udall, who has a bill with myself. He's a Democrat from New Mexico, and I think he'd accept the label progressive. He and I have a bill together to end the Afghan war, end the authorization of force within a year, and actually give our troops a sizable bonus, $2,500 a piece. And that still would save money because we saved $50 billion the first year, even with the bonuses, we'd save about $45 billion. And so there are good Democrats on this, but it's harder and harder. The Trump derangement syndrome, the hate for Trump is so bad, particularly in the media and with some Democrats, not those aren't the same thing. But it's such that they can't see beyond or get back to what they used to always for. Now, when was Obama was in there, happy to have me supporting policies. They supported with their president. But if the reverse is difficult for them because they're such a hatred for Trump. Yeah. And I asked that and I'm going to pivot to police reform and criminal justice because if nothing else, the murder of George Floyd and Breonna Taylor and other similar events should have been a catalyst to do some substantial reforms in the Congress and you stepped up very early with the Justice for Breonna Taylor Act and you've been talking about over militarization of police. I think you've introduced legislation on that as well. For years, and you've been a consistent voice since you got to the Senate, Mike Lee has been a consistent voice, and you have had bipartisan cooperation with a number of people. But for all of the heat and all of the protests, and now a lot of the violence that it's devolved into, there's been no movement on criminal justice or police reform. And I hate to be a bearer of partisan news, but this is the Democrat side that didn't want any. So for example, you can easily look at the Republican proposal and say, this is weak sauce. There's a much there. And that's a valid criticism. But they just voted no to even get on the bill. I probably wouldn't have voted for the Republican bill because there were some things I've objected to, but I voted to get on the bill. and then let's see what happens now the republican leadership often isn't you know completely honest about it to tell you the truth they often don't allow amendments but had they not allowed any of my amendments or the democrat amendments then you go to get off and you play in the republicans and actually got a solid case then you play the republicans for not allowing amendments i think there would have been significant pressure on the republicans to allow amendments and several of those amendments would have passed like the no knock uh... raid bill you know getting rid of no knock raids i think would have passed i think militarization of the uh... getting rid of militarization like bayonets for the police and tanks and all this stuff i think that would have passed also And in the end, Democrats said, every one of them, they all voted together and said, we're not getting on the bill. We'll campaign on it in November. And so there's a certain cockiness. You know, they think they're going to beat Trump and then they also think they're going to take the Senate. They might. But then they can set the agenda if they get rid of the filibuster, which they might do that also. But for right now, they're not in a talking mood. Same's going on with like this more COVID bailout money. The Democrats want more money. The Republicans want more money. They only dispute between these two groups of big government people is how much. But right now the Democrats are holding off doing anything because I think they would like to see maybe there's campaign on Republicans won't give us enough free money. Sure. And I remember that dynamic when I was working on criminal justice reform and there was this trans-partisan coalition going into the 2016 election, some of my progressive friends would openly say, We don't want to give a Republican a win, particularly on this issue. This is our issue. And I guess the assumption was that Hillary Clinton would win, but it's kind of a wicked tribalism that prevents us from actually fixing And sometimes we get beyond it. We did one small first step, the first step back on criminal justice reform. And it was good. It was it was somewhat weak sauce, but it did some good things. And we got that done under under President Trump, you know, with the Republican Senate and the Democrat House. But for the eight years of Obama, for the six out of the eight that I was here, I would go to the White House, I was willing to meet and have my picture taking with President Obama, who I didn't agree with on a lot, to be for criminal justice reform, but we never got it done. And, you know, President Trump's credit, we actually did get it done. I think we need to get more done, but it was the first step. I also appreciate for those who don't think people should be in jail forever, that he commuted the sentence of like Alice Marie Johnson, who somebody who really among a lot of people really did deserve not to be in prison and probably never should have been there that long. But he has done some good things on these issues and he could do more. But it's why I try to stay where I can continue to try to push him in the direction that I think is a good direction. And I think he has come in our direction quite a bit more so than maybe his natural instincts were five, 10 years ago. Yeah, I mean, you look at some of his rhetoric from 16. I think he's definitely thought about it more and heard some other voices and more thoughtful on this. And I do think it's sort of counterintuitive. But right now, a lot of Democrats are the roadblocks. And some of your Republican colleagues, like Tom Cotton, say we have an under incarceration problem, but I suspect he's he's fortunately a minority in our caucus now. There's only two or three of the voices like that in our caucus. And most of our voices have kind of gone in the opposite direction. And so I think we're actually doing better and better on criminal justice reform. And I think we could keep doing it. I think we could do a, you know, first step act revisited or a second step act. I think we really could do something again. But we're not going to do it in the middle of this campaign season right now. It's going to have to happen afterwards. And, you know, if Republicans lose the White House, we have to agree to keep working on this. But frankly, people who are thinking about who to vote for, do you remember Biden won any great hero of less incarceration of African Americans or people using drugs? Biden will throw the book at him and He was going to get rid of all these terrible super predators, which really I would think from a certain perspective must seem very obnoxious, you know, for him to use that language. But I don't know. I think we we still have a chance, but unfortunately we have a chance for almost nothing before the election other than shoveling money. And I've told people we have no money. We have no rainy day account. We have no savings account. It's not like I can walk over to the Federal Reserve and open this big Big, safe, and out. Oh, there's all his money. Why don't we get envelopes and stick all his money and envelopes and send it to people? But we're doing it, but we don't have any money. It's all borrowed or created. So I have this theory and you feel free to pull me off the ledge, but Nancy Pelosi wants to spend another three trillion that we don't have. Mitch McConnell wants to spend a trillion that we don't have. And I think the compromise position in the middle is going to be four to five trillion. Exactly. Yeah. The only compromise that ever happens around Washington and actually happens all the time is to spend money and tomorrow money. And people think, oh, I'm Washington, there's no compromise and the right and the left are so far apart. No, they're not. It's actually the opposite of what you think. There's too much compromise in Washington. It's all about spending money we don't have. But in our caucus last week, Republican Senator stood up and he said, the conservative positions are trillion. I'm not going beyond a trillion. You know, I'm willing to borrow another trillion. This is after we've already borrowed three trillion. And we're $1 trillion in the hole just for normal expenses. Normal everyday budgeting, a trillion in the hole. We have added three, so we're $4 trillion in the hole. Now we're talking about trillion and like the conservative position is a trillion. But we have to spend a trillion because you know what? We have to get elected and we have all these people and the people in the battleground states have to go home and say that they got free money for their people so they can get elected. But if they don't get elected, you know the Democrats will spend even more. So a trillion is now the conservative position and another trillion in borrowing. Yeah. And I think Nancy Pelosi knows that. And that's why I think you're going to get four or five because she's going to. Right. They either completely cave or Republicans finally get a backbone and they stand up and then they bludgeon them with it. But I've also said the people who believe you're in a battleground state that you're going to win. And this always happens. You see them as they get closer and closer. You see this look of fear. I might have to go into the private sector. Oh, I might have to have a real job. Oh, no, I might have to get up in the morning and put a coat and tie on and go to work. And they're like, what do I do to get elected? I'm going to vote like the Democrats. I'm going to vote for as much money as I can pass out. And so you see them now. If you'll watch the voting trends and voting records, those are out for election. Boom, boom, boom. They're all going to the other side, but they don't realize that. the conservative base where I came from including the libertarian conservative base we hated the bank bailouts that's why the tea party started that was this whole thing that got going and we're not that excited about it it's like we have a choice we could stay home or we could vote for the libertarian candidate but both both choices are out there and in in a close state Even if three percent of your hardcore conservatives that are the backbone of the party, either vote for the libertarian or stay home, you lose. Every one of these five senators who's now think they have to pass out all kinds of free money could lose because they lose a conservative base. It's frustrating that it's sort of like the movie Groundhog Day where I feel like the voters have taught Republicans that less than over and over again, you know, starting in 2010, but you could go back to 1994 that being actually different from the Democrats, being fiscal conservatives, defending the Constitution, these are winning issues. But when you split the difference on the Democrats, bad ideas, like, why would people show up for you? You know, I think the my hope is the one good thing that could come out of this is we're beginning to fight in the springtime. Nobody was even here. I came back for one of the votes, but I didn't I would have voted no, but I didn't force the vote to be recorded because I didn't want to force some of our older senators to come back and be blamed if one of them should die. And I mean, I have sympathy for people and this is a bad disease. And so I didn't force them to come back. Now we're all back. I can't be accused of, you know, in the spring they were accusing me of trying to kill everyone anyway because I had this virus. But they can't accuse me of that now. I will force votes on this and there's beginning to solidify some opposition to this. There is a remnant of conservatism in the party. It's small and in the elected officials, but people are starting to rally. My hope is that maybe it could be the beginning of like a Tea Party movement again where people get frustrated with the big government Republicans. And the media never quite got this. They're like, oh, you hate President Obama. That's why the Tea Party started as I won't know it started before him. And it really started in response to Republicans. You were there. It started in response to big government Republicans, not really to Obama at all. thing about Pelosi's bill, and I suspect that there's some version of it in McConnell's bill as well, that really irks me. And by the way, almost everything in that legislation is garbage and creates perverse incentives. But the bailout of primarily blue states, primarily New York and California, who were bankrupt before all this started, who so aggressively locked down their economies And of course, that predictably resulted in them just completely draining their state treasury. So like if you don't allow businesses to do business, they can't pay taxes. And now we're going to bail them out. And that strikes me as rewarding the worst kind of behavior. rewarding bad behavior, but also encouraging more bad behavior. So right now they don't have any income. They can get it one of two ways. We can give it to them, or they can open up their economy for business when people start paying taxes again. If you give them money, they're going to be less likely to open their economy again. So everything we do to give either people money we don't have, or state's money, all is a disincentive to opening. And the hysteria over not opening the economy or not opening the schools isn't based on science. They say they want to be science evidence evidence based, etc. But the science really is on the side of opening and the science is that really there are a bunch of age groups here that really aren't at risk kids goes under 45. The death rates less than seasonal flu above age 45 more than the seasonal flu and as you go up quite a bit more. It's dangerous for older people, and it's not very dangerous for young people. But they have this collectivist notion that, well, it's your responsibility then. Everybody should wear a mask to protect older people. But we never used to have that. I mean, how many people do you think are on chemotherapy for cancer right now? 100,000, 200,000, pretty many. We're a country of 330 million people. And it's sad. I mean, I've made great sympathy for people who are going through that. But we've never thought that everybody in society should wear a mask for people who are on chemotherapy. That's essentially what we're arguing for now is that everybody should wear a mask for those who are at risk. Instead of warning those at risk not to probably go to baseball games, not to go to a big church service. But if you're 25 years old and you have a kid that's two and three, going to church probably says absolutely no risk to you at all. If we're going to talk about relative risks, it strikes me that cancer is 10 times the killer that COVID is. And it's the second largest killer of any disease. And all of these mandates, particularly on hospitals, have prevented patients from getting treatment. People have tough time sort of thinking about risk and public policy. I mean, if you look at how many people die from falls versus terrorism, falling is way worse problem. And if we just put some guardrails on the ladders and, you know, elevators on the ladders and, you know, helmets for people going up ladders, but we don't do all that. We spend almost no money on that. We spend all this money on terrorism, trillions of dollars, but falling is much, much more dangerous than terrorism. Yeah. So I watched your last exchange with Dr. Fauci, and I have an idea for you. If you actually want the public schools to open again, we should stop paying teachers for not teaching. Oh gosh, you're so mean. You would not pay people. You would not pay people who don't work. How mean it's my like I live unfortunate I live in this town Washington DC and it really is starting to get under my skin that no one in this town seems to be nervous about the fact that we just had the worst economic quarter. in my lifetime, I think perhaps in history, because the machine, the government employee ecosystem, including all of the sort of private interests that feed off of it, they're all fine right now. And at some point, they won't be, but it strikes me that teachers would be more interested in actually doing something reasonable if they actually had skin in the game. Yeah, they would. But there's absolutely no backbone, zero backbone for actually saying you won't pay people who refuse to work. There's another choice that would actually have a chance and they're even trying to stop that too. And that would be that private schools would stay open and guess what? People get frustrated by having no education in public schools and people would flood over to them. So the public schools are now trying to get the public health people to prevent private schools from being open too. And they did it in Montgomery County over in Maryland. But I think the governor's now reversed it and we'll see what happens with that. But it'll be a great experiment. Unfortunately, not everybody can do it. Now I'd be for vouchers and let everybody do it. In fact, I have an amendment to the bill coming up to the, if there's a COVID relief bill that comes up. There's something called Title I money comes out of the Department of Education. I'm not for the Department of Education or having the federal government involved, but they are involved and they pass out money to school districts all across the country. What I'd like to do is say that that money follows a student wherever the student goes. So it's allotted per capita to each student. The student wants to be taught at home that money follows a student to the parents who can buy computers and things for homeschooling. They want to go to private school, goes to a private school, and it also goes to whichever public school they want to. So even if we're choosing them on public schools in Louisville, We have a poor area of town and the schools aren't doing so well. We have a richer area of town. Schools are doing better. Those kids ought to be able to take their money and go across town if they can figure out how to get across town and go to a better school. And I would completely transform education because competition works. Competition breeds excellence. If actually letting parents spend their own money on their children, I know freedom and that's so antithetical, you know, allow people free choice. But I think it does have potential. And conservatives haven't done enough with promoting school choice and what it will do. And conservatives often aren't good at showing that they empathize with people who have a rough life. And there have been people who have said that education is the real civil rights issue of our era. And I believe that. And the people against letting you get a good education have been often the teachers union who don't want to give you choice. They hate even public charter schools, much less private charter schools, but give people choice. The thing the teachers ought to realize is that guess what? If you're a good teacher, it's actually going to bid up your salary. If you have more school choices and people can go to a private school and private says, you're one of the best teachers. Why don't you come over to our school? That's a bidding war for your services. If you're a good teacher, the salaries will go up. But I don't know. We've failed at getting that moving and often even Republicans have been divided over school choice. It kind of goes back to that, I mean, I call it haves versus have-nots, but the beltway bubble, and there's a bubble in every city where if you're wealthy enough, and your school is lousy, you can just opt out and go buy an alternative thing. And parents that are stuck with the school's clothes, and maybe they have two jobs right now, they're the ones that suffer. It's not my beltway neighbors. Well, and the other people to blame are frankly Dr. Fauci and others who are spreading hysteria. So when he's come into me and say, well, he's unsure and he's just not, oh, whoa is me, we couldn't do this. I say, well, the evidence shows that children rarely get this, rarely transmit it and rarely die from this. So the mortality is about one in a million or less for those under age 18, under age 18, very low, but they've also done contact tracing in four different countries. Country-wide contact tracing in countries that did a lot of contact tracing, they find rare if any transmission from children up. You say, well, why would that be? That sounds doesn't make any sense. The reason is you find rare transmission from asymptomatic people, whether they're kids or adults. Why because if you're not coughing and hacking up stuff you aren't spreading it so I had it and I was asymptomatic my wife didn't get it. That's not a study, but it just shows you that it isn't always that easy to get and It is more some people end up getting really sick and coughing and stuff They're very contagious and can spread it to a lot of people But the kids don't now they're saying we have to test every kid You know and then they got to wear a mask and test them again at the end of the day The bottom line is is you know what I would do? I'd open every school, and I would wait and see if anybody gets sick. If you have a bunch of people get sick, you'll test in that school, and guess what? You might have to close that school. That's what we did with the flu forever. We based it on people being sick, but now we're basing it on the non-sick. All the rules and restrictions are on those who aren't ill, not necessarily those who are ill. Your opening statement at the Fauci hearing was a tour de force of sort of Hayek 101, and you had just a couple minutes to make that argument. And I would add something to it. So we, you know, we talk about following the science and following the experts and, and doing what health professionals tell us to do, but Fauci is not a health professional. He's a government worker and he's been in there some extraordinary amount of time. So whether we like it or not, he's, he's a political animal and you can see it in his statements because he wouldn't answer your questions and, and he's taken every position on masks as far as I can tell. Right. One of the best tweets I saw the other day was that he seems to have an opinion on Tinder sex and baseball, but when it comes to whether or not you should march in a protest, whether that might be risky to your health, no opinion at all, refuses to comment on whether or not if you're out in a big city with hundreds of other people protesting racism. In fact, there were actually people I think at the WHO who responded and said, Well, if the cause is righteous, like protesting against racism, surely you can't catch the disease at those. And it's like, that just shows how political this is. It's a little medieval or something. Yeah. Yeah, really. You know, if your motive is good, you can't catch this disease. But I don't know. I don't think he's a bad person. I don't think his motives are bad. But he is a government doctor. He's only seen the government perspective and he's an incredibly cautious person. And so he's unwilling, you know, that's my point. Well, if we're going to talk about whether kids to go to school, shouldn't we bring up some statistics? Childhood mortality, shouldn't we discuss that? And even that, he didn't really want to discuss that because it is very, very unusual. The WHO had a scientist who came and she broadcast that. She said, according to this study, children are rarely getting this and rarely transmitting it. She linked to a scientific paper. I don't know if it was hers or someone else, but she's a scientist from the WHO. She's not from the right. She's not she is a government scientist. She says this within a day. They made her apology. Mal type apology. I'm sorry. They had a sort of a struggle session with her and then she apologizes. I'm so sorry. I spoke against the state, but then they took down the link. And so if you go to her press conference, you can find her still. Maybe you don't find her anymore, but for a while you could find her, but the link to the WHO study was taken down, and it's amazing all the stuff that's out there, all the stuff is being taken down. And I'm not for having the government get involved, but it doesn't mean I'm not annoyed by all the stuff on hydroxychloroquine. Twitter has decided that they are the preeminent scientists on Earth, and you can't, if you