. And that's a nice segue to the final question. I know you've got to get going, but I saw your tweet storm about Portland and the Trump administration's decision to send some mysterious federal agent force in there. And I don't know about you, but I've been watching Portland devolve into chaos for years now. It seems like the last three to five years, there's been violence in the streets every day. And what's your take on what we shouldn't do in Portland? I have a great deal of sympathy for those who are upset about riding, destruction and chaos in anarchy in Portland and in other cities, frankly. I think if you want it to be different, it's important to know how we got here and whose fault it is. Portland's been run by Democrats for 50 years, Minneapolis run by Democrats for 50 years, Louisville. We have problems in our city in Louisville and Kentucky, last Republican mayor in 1972. I think when you send the federal government in and Trump gets in the middle of that, Trump becomes the target. So just for purely political reasons, I think it's a bad idea because you're taking blame away from the people who deserve to have the blame, the Democrats who have been running our cities. On another note, though, whether or not we should have a federal police force, the Constitution doesn't provide for a federal police force. Our Founding Fathers didn't even like a standing army, much less sending police in the communities. Our Founding Fathers would not even be able to fathom having a federal police official from Washington, D.C., go to Portland. Law enforcement was always local, always has been, always should be. Policing needs to be local too, and the reason policing ought to be local and not federal, even when you're upset. And look, I'm as upset as anybody else about Portland. I think it's terrible, and I would want to do everything we can do to try to prevent it if I lived in Portland. And yet, the job of a policeman is very difficult. A policeman has to keep the peace, keep people from killing each other, arrest people who are trying to invade the rights or space of other individuals, but they also have to protect the First Amendment and the Bill of Rights. And so in our country, you are allowed to stand up and hold your sign up. You're allowed to stand up and say, big brother sucks. You're allowed to do that. And there's a terrible picture and I'm gonna ask the head of Department of Homeland Security this week about it. There's a video going around of a guy and I'm not sure if he's holding up a music speaker or if he's holding up a sign and you see him and they shoot like a canister towards him and I think he throws it back but he's in no way aggressive, he's not armed, doesn't look like he's committed any violence and he's shot in the head with one of these rubber bullets which are pretty potent rubber bullets. They don't kill you, but it opens skin and fracture to skull. And I think he's had to have reconstructive surgery and it's like, you know, nobody deserves that. And I think a local policeman is a little bit more aware of what they're supposed to do and not do. And I always kind of say this when I go around Kentucky, I appreciate the local police come to my rallies to help me, to protect me from crazy people. They usually just kind of are standing there, but they're a deterrent for us. And I usually say, you know what, the local police do understand the Fourth Amendment because they know they always have to, they're pretty comfortable with it. They have to ask a judge before they go to someone's house. They have to have a warrant. So I think local police are more able to understand the rules of the Fourth Amendment, the bill of rights. And so I worry about having a lot of federal police. You know, another thing we're concerned about when we're looking into is these police are coming from the department. of Homeland Security and the Customs and Border Agents. We've learned that within 100 miles of the border, they can do a lot of things, deportations, arrests, things that do not necessarily have constitutional protection within 100 miles of the border. So you know how they say we have to guard the border against people coming in? That's fine. The border is 100 miles. We've looked at the population in the United States, it involves all of our coastal cities. Yeah. So within 100 miles of the border, I think my staff told me it's over 80% of the US population, but they have sort of these military sort of powers within that. And so there's a movement on to try to either make that smaller or do something. And I think there's one proposal to make it 25 miles. My point is, well, most cities will be within 25 miles of the coastline. I'm not sure it's that much better. Really if there's going to be a sort of a war zone where you can arrest people who are invading your country, I would think the first couple hundred yards of someone coming in illegally would be a reasonable area where there might be a little more leeway for police. But 25 miles or 100 miles is very worrisome. They're also using in Portland. Some of these people are custom and this isn't really a border. These aren't people invading across the border. They're using the excuse of that to be able to have a justification for their police force. Yeah, that's a wildly scary precedent where everything becomes federal. Right. And you can see the reverse of this. Trump is doing this because of mayhem and rioting, which I think a lot of us are sympathetic to stopping that. You could see how it could be used to send in, you know, police to confiscate guns. You could see how it could be have people send in to confiscate books. What if you're writing God forbid? But what if you were writing books saying hydroxychloroquine works? I can see them wanting to take those books because those could be so dangerous to public thought. That's a depressing way to end this. What's the one-liner happy news? Here's my happy news. You were talking earlier, is there a happy channel you can turn to on television? Well, there isn't. But if I want to think about the world in a positive way, I'd like to go to humanprogress.org. And it's a website, Miriam 2P. Is that right? Yep. 2P. Yeah. It writes this website. I'm a big fan of his. And there's just so much good news when you put it in perspective. And this is true also of things that right now what i don't like about the the race consciousness is there by saying it's a terrible place in america's a terrible place where their blemishes in our history obviously did we do things wrong in the past but compared to any other time in american history or world history This is the best of all possible worlds. There's less racism, less discrimination than there's ever been in any time in the world. There's also more wealth than there's ever been. And the reason I think we shouldn't get too distraught about race and making everything about race is I really, if I'm talking to a hundred young African American men and women who are like graduating from college, I'm going to tell them the world's your oyster baby. This is a great place. Public corporations, everybody, is there still some bad people out there? Yes. But guess what? There's never been a better time to be hired and employed in advance in the public world than there's ever been based. There really is no more of this systemic racism in big business or in government. You know, we've gotten beyond the terrible people like Woodrow Wilson, who brought racism into government hiring. We're in so much better place, but there is a downside to saying everybody hates everybody and we're in a terrible place because it's a self-fulfilling prophecy that everybody's going to talk about nothing but that. And instead, you know, I don't know. I just see so many good things going on, but humanprogress.org looks at a lot of this economic statistics and shows how much better off than we have been. And this is for all races, but how much better off we've been in a long, long time. Okay. You single-handedly saved this episode. Thank you, Senator. All right. Thank you. Thanks for watching Kibbe on Liberty. By now, you know this is the most important event of your week. So make sure you subscribe on YouTube, click the little bell so you get notifications. Kibbe on Liberty, mostly honest conversations with mostly interesting people.",
